]]}, {"t": " Arc", "lp": -0.14745955169200897, "top": [[" Arc", -0.14745955169200897], [" driver", -2.498037815093994]]}, {"t": " driver", "lp": -0.46989110112190247, "top": [[" driver", -0.46989110112190247], [" drivers", -1.3126016855239868]]}, {"t": " configurations", "lp": -0.7300209403038025, "top": [[" configurations", -0.7300209403038025], [" documentation", -1.7125571966171265]]}], "text": "Based on current technical documentation and driver behavior for Intel Arc GPUs (including the A770) under the Linux `xe` driver (or the newer `intel-mesa`/`i915` drivers), here is the breakdown:\n\n### 1. **CCS Mode 1 vs. Mode 2**\n\n**CCS (Constant Color Surface)** modes refer to how the GPU handles **color space conversions** and **gamma correction** during rendering, particularly when using **YUV** or **HDR** content.\n\n- **CCS Mode 1**:  \n  - **Standard sRGB / Linear workflow**.  \n  - Used for **SDR (Standard Dynamic Range)** content.  \n  - Applies standard gamma correction (typically 2.2 or sRGB curve).  \n  - **Default mode** for most desktop compositing and video playback.  \n  - **Use when**: You are displaying normal SDR content (e.g., YouTube videos, desktop UI, games in SDR).\n\n- **CCS Mode 2**:  \n  - **HDR (High Dynamic Range) workflow**.  \n  - Enables **linear color space** handling with **extended dynamic range**.  \n  - Requires **HDR-capable display** and **HDR metadata** (e.g., PQ, HLG).  \n  - **Use when**: You are playing HDR video (e.g., Netflix HDR, YouTube HDR) or using HDR games with proper HDR metadata.  \n  - **Note**: On Intel Arc, this mode is often **auto-selected** by the compositor (e.g., KDE Plasma 6, GNOME 45+) when HDR content is detected. Manual selection is rare.\n\n### 2. **When to Use CCS Mode 4?**\n\n**CCS Mode 4** is **not a standard user-selectable mode** in typical Intel Arc driver configurations"}